1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,716
How to tackle case ENG1M-0000001.

2
00:00:03,716 --> 00:00:10,142
A step-by-step method you can repeat on any case like this one.

3
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:24,124
Cases like this follow the operations & engineering improvement model: Baseline KPIs, then bottleneck/root cause, then optimize/simulate, then pilot, then ROI + KPIs.

4
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:33,957
Start with one question: How do we achieve 8.5% capacity scaling improvement within 3 months and 866 thousand dollars?

5
00:00:33,957 --> 00:00:42,045
Split it into three branches with no overlap: Demand & mix, Capacity at the constraint, Flow between stages.

6
00:00:42,045 --> 00:00:45,040
Each person on the team owns one branch.

7
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:50,758
Within twenty minutes, write down a hypothesis you can prove wrong.

8
00:00:50,758 --> 00:00:59,912
Here: The gap is one binding constraint, not total capacity: relieving it delivers the target without rebuilding the whole system.

9
00:00:59,912 --> 00:01:02,659
If the data kills it, pivot and say so.

10
00:01:02,659 --> 00:01:04,208
Judges reward honesty.

11
00:01:05,210 --> 00:01:08,256
Use only the tools that fit.

12
00:01:08,256 --> 00:01:12,717
Theory of Constraints (5 focusing steps).

13
00:01:12,717 --> 00:01:17,396
OEE = Availability × Performance × Quality.

14
00:01:17,396 --> 00:01:22,074
Little's Law: WIP = Throughput × Flow time.

15
00:01:22,074 --> 00:01:29,037
Then do the quick math: Gap: 1,190.00 , then 1,291.15 units/day.

16
00:01:29,037 --> 00:01:34,586
Budget per % point of target: 102 thousand dollars.

17
00:01:35,590 --> 00:01:44,107
Score all three options with explicit weights: target attainment, return, payback, downside resilience and budget headroom.

18
00:01:44,107 --> 00:01:48,262
The budget is a hard gate: an option over budget cannot win.

19
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:51,568
Then build a solution ladder.

20
00:01:51,568 --> 00:01:53,557
Level one, the quick win.

21
00:01:53,557 --> 00:01:57,775
Level two, the cheapest option that meets the target.

22
00:01:57,775 --> 00:02:00,401
Level three, the value maximiser.

23
00:02:00,401 --> 00:02:02,311
Level four, the stretch.

24
00:02:02,311 --> 00:02:07,404
Your recommendation should explain where it sits on this ladder.

25
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:11,288
Finally, protect your time.

26
00:02:11,288 --> 00:02:14,175
0:00 to 0:40, Read & align.

27
00:02:14,175 --> 00:02:17,598
0:40 to 1:50, Data & quick math.

28
00:02:17,598 --> 00:02:21,127
1:50 to 3:20, Options & decision.

29
00:02:21,127 --> 00:02:23,159
3:20 to 4:20, Deck.

30
00:02:23,159 --> 00:02:26,261
4:20 to 5:00, Rehearse & Q&A.

31
00:02:26,261 --> 00:02:31,608
Video three shows the answer this method produces.
